WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.200
Let’s talk about keeping the faith.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.080
Celebrating births and marriages

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.560
mourning the dead

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.440
coming together to mark the holidays.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.040
They've all been derailed.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.680
Sacred sites and places of worship have been closed.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.280
No big festivals,  no shared meals 

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:38.800
no proper funerals.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.560
Whether you're religious or not.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.360
So how are people coping?

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.360
How do we maintain our spiritual

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.440
and cultural practices under lockdown?

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.680
And what can we all do to stay strong?

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.120
Some of us choose to navigate through life on our own.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.600
But the vast majority of people turn to religion

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.720
for spiritual sustenance, direction and community.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.680
Especially when times are hard.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.240
And this pandemic is one of those times.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.960
Over 85% of the world's population

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.560
considers religion very important in their lives.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.800
And so when they cannot gather

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.520
as a result of the social restrictions of COVID-19

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.520
it feels like an incredible loss.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.120
Governments around the world have banned religious

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.000
and spiritual gatherings to contain the pandemic.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.160
And with good reason.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:35.360
Probably the most notorious example

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:39.280
of what they’re trying to avoid is the story of Patient 31.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.320
In early February a woman in South Korea

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.560
developed a fever and went to hospital.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.480
She was tested for COVID-19

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.600
but before her results came back

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.560
she attended at least four church services.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.320
She even went for a buffet lunch.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.760
By the start of March more than 4,000 people

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:01.120
were infected — 60% of them were from her church.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.040
Easy to see though how just one person

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.760
can become a super-spreader.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.120
April is a big month for religious holidays

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.880
but this year things had to change.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.440
Jews who would normally share meals

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.720
and prayers for Passover were forced to congregate online.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:37.320
“I’m here to welcome all of you to our Zoom Pesach.” 

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:38.840
Instead of celebrating mass

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.840
before tens of thousands of worshippers

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.240
in Saint Peter’s Square

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.560
Pope Francis livestreamed his Palm Sunday sermon.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.680
Sikhs usually hold the festival Vaisakhi.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.680
This year they did it at a safe distance.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:03:00.040
There were other celebrations,  too, like Orthodox Easter

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:04.240
and a Hindu festival called Rama Navami.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:07.680
And now Ramadan

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.280
a month of fasting considered holy

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:17.400
by 2 billion Muslims around the world.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.720
People won't gather together for breaking of the fast

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:26.040
to pray in the evenings and they won’t gather together

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.120
when it comes to Eid, where people come together

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.280
to celebrate the end of the month of Ramadan.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:33.200
We all plan to have a big Zoom Eid party

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.280
dress nice and have fun.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.160
It’s going to be a little more different than usual

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:38.920
but I think we’ll still have a good time.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.440
The faithful around the world

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:41.960
no matter what their religion

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.280
are having to adjust.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.520
And congregations are getting creative.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:53.920
Families are re-enacting rituals at home.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.840
Many are taking advantage of technology.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.280
Or tuning into more traditional channels.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.760
Every Sunday now if you want to attend a mass

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.600
you just turn on your TV and that’s also good. 

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:14.200
That said not everyone has found it easy to adapt.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:17.320
My parents, they seem to be having a hard time.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:22.320
One of the ways to cope with it is their faith

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.560
and just putting everything in God's hands but right now

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.640
you know, that’s been a struggle.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.920
Others just can’t afford the tech.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.920
Those groups that are least resourced may also be

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:35.560
the groups that most need religion.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:37.640
And it goes beyond the spiritual.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:39.880
Some people rely on their religious community

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.160
to make sure they get enough to eat.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.160
They might provide economic benefit for people.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.640
And they of course provide emotional

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.200
and psychological support.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.040
And there’s probably no more difficult time

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:53.160
than losing someone.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:54.480
The pandemic has forced

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.560
restrictions on funerals and burials

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.160
that defy our universal rituals of saying goodbye.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:07.680
Some cultures are adapting traditional ceremonies.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:16.960
This farewell to a prominent Maori leader

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:19.760
was broadcast on national television in New Zealand.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.440
But for a lot of people saying goodbye from a distance

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:31.520
or online just isn’t enough.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:36.080
As a family, being together with her would have helped us

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:38.680
grieve together for a moment as well

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:43.480
which helps you also acknowledge the enormity

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:44.360
of what’s happened.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.680
It still feels like I’m going to have to wake up

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.440
tomorrow and ring people and tell them I got it wrong

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:50.840
and that she’s still alive.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:12.320
All of this puts spiritual

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.840
and religious leaders in a tough spot.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:17.520
Many want to provide comfort to their followers

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:19.360
but they’re also obliged to stop them

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:20.760
from coming together.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:22.480
And that goes against centuries

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:23.840
of religious practices.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:27.760
Historically I can't imagine any imam, any leader of a mosque

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:29.760
ever having a situation where they have to

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.280
close the doors of the mosque, which is supposed to be

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.440
a sanctuary at times of difficulty.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.080
The vast majority of religious leaders and scholars

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:39.480
around the world are leading by example.

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:42.080
But some are breaking the rules.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:44.000
Remember the story about Patient 31?

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:47.520
“Some local leaders have called for Shincheonji’s elders

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:50.080
to be investigated for possible homicide.”

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.120
Several church leaders are accused of

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:54.240
hiding the identities of their followers

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:55.560
from South Korean officials

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:58.120
trying to track down cases of COVID-19.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:04.120
Other religious communities have ignored lockdowns too.

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:09.720
Large weddings by Hasidic Jews went ahead in New York.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:13.360
Some Muslims in Malaysia kept going to Friday prayers.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:16.520
Mega-churches in US states like Florida

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.640
and Louisiana were still packed.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:22.120
Bless them, bless them. Put a holy boldness on them.

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:23.960
And it’s caused a backlash.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:25.840
For example an Islamic group in India

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:28.400
held an event against police orders.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:30.960
That led to a surge in anti-Muslim violence.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.880
It’s a trying time for a lot of people.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:36.320
None of us have ever lived through

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:37.680
anything like this before.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:39.160
Closing more public spaces.

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.280
“Forcibly separated from loved ones.”

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.360
Around 100,000 people have died.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:45.920
World powers that are armed for the world’s biggest wars

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:48.000
are now fighting for basic protective gear.

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:52.600
Lockdowns are already taking a toll on

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:54.800
our spiritual and mental well-being.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:01.000
Everybody is having a lot of fear and panic, anxiety.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:05.400
We shouldn't underemphasise the collateral impact

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:08.200
that COVID-19 social restrictions will have on

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:10.480
our mental health and on our psyche.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:13.040
So in absence of a physical connection

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:14.840
people are coping the best they can.

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:18.560
Utilise technology because getting together and meeting

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:21.200
is really important especially in times like these.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:33.120
It's not from outside. It's from here.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:34.080
What you feel here.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.760
Just keep believing, keep praying for the health workers

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:41.080
for those who are sick and for yourselves

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:42.520
who are sitting at home safe.

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:46.560
It’s in difficult times like this that people turn to their faith

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:48.600
and to each other for support.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:53.400
It shows just how precious our sense of community really is.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:57.800
“We don’t know when we’ll be back on

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.080
the Start Here set but not to worry.

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:02.360
We’re still going to be bringing you more episodes

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:03.560
any way we can.

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.920
In the meantime our website aljazeera.com

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:08.880
has all the latest on the coronavirus pandemic.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:09.880
I’ll see you next week.”

